\b Step 1 - \b0\i0\ulnone \i Pipeline architecture diagram: describe the raw data and its source.\b0\i0\ulnone \b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The raw data is stored in one CSV file called synthetic_clinical_dataset.csv, which is defined in the config in data.raw_path. The data includes 10,000 rows (one per patient) and 14 columns in total. Six columns are integers, six are decimals, and two are text (sex and diagnosis). The data profile shows no missing values and no duplicate rows. The input variables for the tool are age, sex, bmi, systolic and diastolic blood pressure, glucose, cholesterol, creatinine, diabetes and hypertension. Diagnosis is a text column that stays in the cleaned file but is not used as an input. The target variable is readmission_30d, indicating an average of about 14% of patients were readmitted within 30 days. Mortality is a competing endpoint and would leak information if used as an input variable. The patient_id variable is a label and thus not used as an input variable. The data profile also reveals a problem that will surface later. Age has a minimum value of 0, meaning some records must be for newborns or children. The tool is designed to work with adult inpatients and thus the ingest filters on age.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i \par
\b Step 2 - \b0\i0\ulnone \i Implement a repeatable process for loading the raw data from a file, URL or other defined source.\b0\i0\ulnone \b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i One function, tplLoadRawData, loads the raw data and reads its settings from the config, so every run loads the data the same way. data.source determines the data source (i.e., whether it is a file or a web address). For files, the raw data is read from data.raw_path. For web addresses, the raw data is downloaded from data.raw_url to the specified path. The download is saved with a temporary file name. Only when the download has completed is the temporary file name replaced with the final file name. Should the download fail for some reason or the resulting file be empty, the run is terminated with a message indicating the problem with the web address or file specified for the test case. In the run report, each run is described by the data source, the location from which the data was loaded, the time it was loaded, and a short file fingerprint. In this case, a local file was read as specified in the config.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i \par
\b Step 6 - \b0\i0\ulnone \i Identify operational risks, such as unavailable or delayed data.\b0\i0\ulnone \b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The biggest risk is that the raw data for a given file is missing or late. For files the system tries to download, if the file or associated web address is unavailable, or if the file is empty, the system will retry the download a number of times, then stop and provide a clear message. Late data is a gap that currently is not addressed. While load time and a file fingerprint are recorded after ingestion, nothing currently checks how old the file is; as a result, a file could be stale and used without warning. A changed layout is a third risk. If the dataset column changes, the Ingest stops when it can\u8217?t find a column listed in the config.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i \par
\b Step 6 - \b0\i0\ulnone \i Consider cultural safety, data authority and the need for consultation where the data, prediction or proposed use could affect Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, communities, Country or knowledge.\b0\i0\ulnone \b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The dataset lacks a column (or combination of columns) that allows identification of Indigenous people. It is not possible to evaluate how the model is treating Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander patients. The dataset would need to include data so performance can be reported for First Nations patients, and any use would need to demonstrate a collective benefit for that community/communities. Regarding authority to control use of the data, this would sit with the relevant Aboriginal community and their health service (e.g., an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Organisation), as well as the hospital\u8217?s data governance and an ethics committee with appropriate Aboriginal representation.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i \par
\b Step 6 - \b0\i0\ulnone \i Where cultural safety issues are not directly evident in the selected dataset, explain what would still need to be considered before applying the system in a real organisational or community setting.\b0\i0\ulnone \b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i Although there are no issues with cultural safety identified in this data, a real hospital would record a patient\u8217?s self-identified Indigenous status, enabling the model to be checked for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander patients. The organisation would also need to consult with and obtain approval from the local Aboriginal community and their health service on how the data will be collected, stored, and used. Patients and staff must be informed that the model exists, how individuals will be flagged, and who can overturn a patient's flagging, with follow-up actions that are culturally safe (e.g. involving Aboriginal health workers). A review should be set up after the system goes live to identify whether any groups have been under-flagged by the model, and to inform the community and organisation so they can take appropriate action where required.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i \par
\b Step 1 - \b0\i0\ulnone \i Define the problem that the AI system addresses and the prediction or decision it is intended to support.\b0\i0\ulnone \b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i Some patients are discharged from the hospital only to return within 30 days. Such readmissions are often stressful for patients and can be costly for hospitals. By predicting readmission among adult inpatients at discharge, the system supports clinicians' decisions about allocating patient follow-up. They can decide whether a patient needs a phone call, a medication review, or an early appointment with the patient\u8217?s General Practitioner. A missed readmission is more expensive than a false alarm. The data used for this working example is synthetic and thus should not be used for real-world patients.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
