\b Step 6 - \b0\i0\ulnone \i Identify the users, affected stakeholders and people or communities who could benefit from or be harmed by the system.\b0\i0\ulnone \b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i Patients identified as high risk for readmission can benefit from a flag that prompts follow-up that would otherwise be missed. Clinicians can benefit from a consistent prompt to support their clinical judgement. The hospital can benefit from fewer avoidable readmissions and improved bed management through reduced length of stay. However, some patient groups also face potential harm. Patients the model does not identify as at risk may receive less appropriate care than others with similar needs if clinicians rely on the flag. Similarly, patients who are incorrectly flagged as being at risk and undergo additional contact as a result could experience significant anxiety for no reason. This model does not cover children. The ingest summary for this model shows that 204 rows outside of the age range of (18 to 100) were removed. In this data, these are the under-18s, so the model says nothing about them and must not be applied to them. The summary also shows that 116 rows outside of the allowed values for sex (Female, Male) were removed. In this data, these are patients whose sex is recorded as Other. There were too few of them to score fairly, and the model only knows two categories, so the model says nothing about them either and must not be applied to them. Removing them also means any bias against this group could not be detected. Older patients may also be served least well, because small groups give very unreliable scores. The dataset for this model does not have columns for ethnicity or Indigenous status. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and other ethnic communities will not be able to determine whether or not they are receiving different treatment as a result of this model. People in rural and remote areas may also be disadvantaged, as follow-up services and other services required for the model's output may be difficult to access in these locations. Any use of this model would require consultation with the communities of concern and a clear data owner before the model can access patients' health information.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
