\b Step 1 - \b0\i0\ulnone \i Pipeline architecture diagram: describe data ingestion.\b0\i0\ulnone \b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The stage of ingestion is ingest.py. This is the only stage that actually reads the raw file. It reads a file specified in the config (e.g. a local file or a web page) and keeps only the columns listed in the config. It removes duplicates, then rows that are outside of the sex specified in the config and age outside of 18-100. It holds out 5% of the remaining patients as \u8220?new patients\u8221? and writes their answers to a file; it then writes the rest of the cleaned data to a file that the remaining stages read from. For each run of the stages, it prints where the data came from, when it was loaded, and a \u8220?fingerprint\u8221? of the file, so later stages and a human can tell where the data came from.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i \par
\b Step 2 - \b0\i0\ulnone \i Describe how invalid or incomplete data is handled.\b0\i0\ulnone \b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The Ingest stage of the pipeline removes invalid rows and reports how many. Spaces are stripped from string columns, duplicates are removed, and rows are removed where the sex is not Female or Male (116) and outside the age range of 18 to 100 (204), and the counts are printed above. The raw data had no missing values. The pipeline never fills in gaps or \u8216?guesses\u8217? values in any of the stages. The Validate stage checks the file after cleaning and stops the pipeline if there are any problems with the cleaned data. The Validate stage stops at once if the table is empty or a required column is missing. Otherwise it runs the remaining checks together and reports every problem in one message: too few rows, missing values in a required column, a value outside the allowed list for sex, an age outside the range, duplicate rows, and a target that is not 0 or 1. The feature stage also refuses any category that is not on the config list, so bad data is stopped as early as possible before it causes problems later in the pipeline.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i \par
\b Step 6 - \b0\i0\ulnone \i Discuss limitations in the data, features, model and evaluation (the data).\b0\i0\ulnone \b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i There are no date or site columns, so data cannot be split into training and test data for different times or sites. A random split of the data will likely overstate how well the model works in practice. There are no columns for Indigenous status or ethnicity, so fairness can only be assessed by sex and age. The average readmission rate is about 14%. Due to this strongly imbalanced class distribution, the results must be interpreted with caution.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
