\b Target Variable:\b0\i0\ulnone  readmission_30d\par
\par
\i Step 1 - Identify the target variable, whether the task is classification or regression, and the unit for which each prediction is made.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The target is readmission_30d. If the value is 1, the patient was readmitted within 30 days of discharge and 0 if not. This is a binary classification, not a regression. Each prediction is made for one adult inpatient at the point of discharge. The model gives a probability of readmission for that patient, and a flag is raised when it is high enough.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 1 - Define the prediction time: the point at which the prediction would be generated in a real workflow.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i This prediction is made at the time of discharge from hospital, after the clinical team have decided that it is safe for the patient to go home. The model uses data that has already been recorded for the patient: age, sex, BMI, blood pressure, glucose, total cholesterol, creatinine, presence of diabetes and presence of hypertension. A simple flag is then generated to assist clinical staff with any further follow-up required before the patient leaves hospital.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 1 - Identify which inputs would genuinely be available at the prediction time. Information that becomes available only afterwards cannot legitimately be used as a feature.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The following 10 input variables are measured before a patient\u8217?s discharge and can be used for prediction: age, sex, BMI, systolic and diastolic blood pressure, glucose, cholesterol, creatinine, diabetes, and hypertension. The mortality variable is not used in the model, as it is measured only after discharge and would otherwise leak part of the outcome into the data used for prediction. The Patient ID variable is a label and is not used as an input variable; it is also not included in the cleaned data file, whereas the diagnosis variable is included in the cleaned data file but not used as an input variable in the model. All columns that are excluded from the model are listed in the config file and in the config check to prevent them from being added as input variables.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 1 - Pipeline architecture diagram: describe feature engineering and preprocessing.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i Feature engineering sits in features.py. The function creates two features. The first is pulse_pressure, the difference between systolic and diastolic blood pressure. The second is sex_Male, which converts sex to a 0/1 feature where males are 1 and females are 0. The config determines the sex categories, so any values not in the category list will end the run rather than be scored against the first category. The function does not learn anything from the data (i.e., no scaling, no averaging, etc.), so it is safe to use on the training data, the test data, and new patients.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 2 - Create at least two meaningful engineered or transformed features and explain their intended role.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The dfEngineerFeatures builds two features. The first is pulse_pressure, the difference between systolic and diastolic blood pressure. This feature captures some cardiovascular stress not captured by the two individual blood pressure readings. The second feature is sex_Male, a text feature that is converted to a numeric feature (1 for male and 0 for female) in the config. Since logistic regression only uses numeric features, female is the reference value for this feature. This feature is not learned from the data and is set in the config.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 2 - Implement preprocessing through a repeatable pipeline or clearly organised set of scripts.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i Ingest, validate, and feature preprocessing are each split into a single named script: ingest.py, validate.py and features.py. Each script can be run with a single command such as python -m src.ingest. The steps are chained together in dvc.yaml so that dvc repro will run them in the correct order after any changes. All configuration settings are in config.yaml. Each step is run in exactly the same way every time.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\b \ul Data split\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\b Set aside as new patients (data.new_data_fraction):\b0\i0\ulnone  5% of the cleaned rows\par
\b Test share (features.test_size):\b0\i0\ulnone  20% of the remaining rows\par
\b Stratified on the target (features.stratify):\b0\i0\ulnone  True\par
\b Training rows:\b0\i0\ulnone  7356 (80.0% of the 9196 rows used)\par
\b Test rows:\b0\i0\ulnone  1840 (20.0% of the 9196 rows used)\par
\b Overall, as a share of the cleaned data:\b0\i0\ulnone  train 76.0%, test 19.0%, new patients 5.0%\par
\par
\i Step 2 - Ensure that transformations are fitted using training data only where this is required to avoid leakage.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i None of the transformations in this pipeline learn anything from the data. The two engineered features are fixed rules set in the config (a simple subtraction, and a 0/1 category column). There\u8217?s no scaling and no gap filling, so nothing can leak from the test data through this part of the pipeline. The only part that learns anything is the model itself, and it\u8217?s trained on the training rows only. Cross-validation also uses the training rows only, and the test data is scored once, at the end. If scaling or imputation were added later, it would need to be a scikit-learn transform within a pipeline, and fitted on the training data only.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 2 - Identify at least one target-leakage, future-information or train-serve consistency risk and explain how it is controlled.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The main leakage risk is mortality. It\u8217?s a post-discharge measurement, so using it as an input would hand the model the outcome. It\u8217?s listed in excluded_columns in the config for this reason, and the config check would prevent the config from being loaded if an excluded column was also in feature_columns. A further risk here is train-serve mismatch, where patients are prepared differently for new predictions than they were for the data used in training. All uses of the data (training, evaluation, prediction and web service) use a single shared function, dfEngineerFeatures, to prepare the features. This function also checks that all categories are in the config list and stops the run if not.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 6 - Discuss limitations in the data, features, model and evaluation (the features).\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The input file contains 10 single-time-point clinical measures. This dataset omits many well-known readmission risk predictors, such as the number of previous admissions, length of stay, medications, and support at home. Diabetes and hypertension are included as simple binary flags, but no indication of severity or degree of control of either of these conditions is provided. The two engineered features, pulse_pressure and sex_Male, are basic. Sex is restricted to male or female. There are no ethnicity, Indigenous status or location columns, so the model can\u8217?t be checked for whether it treats different groups differently. The data is synthetic, so the model can only learn the relationships embedded in the data generator.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\b \ul Training evidence (from the model card)\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\b Training rows:\b0\i0\ulnone  7356\par
\b Cross-validated AUC:\b0\i0\ulnone  baseline 0.500, model 0.606, gain 0.106, required 0.050, PASSED\par
\par
\i Step 1 - Pipeline architecture diagram: describe model training.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The training stage (train.py), loads and validates the cleaned data, builds the features, splits off the test rows and fits a logistic regression on the 7,356 training rows, using the settings in the model section of the config.yaml file. Before anything can be saved, it must first pass through an approval gate. The cross-validated AUC on the training rows needs to be at least 0.050 above the baseline AUC. The cross-validation AUC for the logistic regression was 0.606 or 0.106 above the baseline value of 0.500. If the gate test fails, the model does not save it. The test ends with an error. A model that passes the test is saved as model.joblib along with a model card (model_card.json) and the run is logged in MLflow.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 1 - Pipeline architecture diagram: describe versioning and experiment or artefact tracking.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i Git versions the code, config.yaml, dvc.yaml, requirements.txt and the raw data, model, metrics and reports. DVC runs the stage graph and records a hash of every input and output in dvc.lock. MLflow tracks the training, evaluation and comparison runs, each tagged with the Git commit and fingerprints of the data and config, and the model card carries the same fingerprints.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\b Required gain:\b0\i0\ulnone  0.050\par
\par
\i Step 1 - Pipeline architecture diagram: describe model saving or approval.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i A model is only saved if it passes the approval gate in train.py. The cross-validated AUC on the training rows needs to be at least 0.050 above the baseline AUC. A model that fails isn\u8217?t saved and the stage stops with an error. A model that passes the test is saved as model.joblib along with a model card (model_card.json). Also, a named person must still approve it before any real use. Passing the gate is an automated check, not clinical approval.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
