\i Step 6 - Explain the consequences of false positives, false negatives or other important model failures in the context of the selected task.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i A false negative is a readmitted patient the model missed, so the patient leaves without the extra monitoring that might have prevented their return. The model missed 99 of the 258 readmissions in the test rows. A false positive is a flagged patient who was not readmitted, which means unnecessary monitoring and clinician time. There were 552 of these, so about 78 percent of flagged patients were false alarms. Recall is traded against precision on the assumption that follow-up is low risk. However, many false alarms would likely swamp a clinical team, so the model should not override a clinician\u8217?s decision given the weak signal in this case.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
