\i Step 1 - Create a pipeline architecture diagram showing: raw data and its source, data ingestion, data validation, feature engineering and preprocessing, model training, model evaluation and benchmarking, versioning and experiment or artefact tracking, model saving or approval, the reusable prediction workflow, and monitoring considerations.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The pipeline starts with the raw data, a synthetic CSV of 10,000 patients, and runs through ingest.py, validate.py (11 checks) and features.py, then train.py, evaluate.py, compare.py and plots.py. Git versions the code, DVC tracks the stage graph, MLflow logs the train, evaluate and compare runs, and each stage writes an RTF report. The model is saved only if its cross-validated AUC beats the baseline by at least 0.05, and a named person has to approve it with approve.py before it can score anyone. predict.py and the FastAPI service run new patients through the same validation and feature code, and monitor.py checks a new batch for input drift, data quality and the share flagged.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\b \ul Why this architecture suits the task\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b Rare outcome:\b0\i0\ulnone  14.0% of training patients were readmitted, so the model uses class_weight = balanced.\par
\par
\b Test rows kept apart:\b0\i0\ulnone  7356 training rows. 20% of the data is held back for testing (stratified: True). Cross-validation uses the training rows only.\par
\par
\b Gate:\b0\i0\ulnone  cross-validated AUC gain over the baseline 0.106, required 0.050, PASSED.\par
