\i Step 3 - Train a simple baseline model, such as a dummy classifier, dummy regressor, historical average, persistence model or existing-rule benchmark.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i In compare.py,  two baselines were added: 1) a dummy classifier that always predicts \u8220?not readmitted\u8221? (since that\u8217?s the most common outcome), and 2) an existing-rule benchmark that looks at age and flags anyone over 65. The dummy classifier has an AUC of 0.500 and finds none of the readmissions (recall 0%) yet it is right 86% of the time. This highlights that accuracy is not a good metric for this problem. The age rule has an AUC of 0.511 and a recall of 32%, meaning age tells us little, and any real model will need to do better than this.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 3 - Train at least one candidate machine learning model.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i Two models were trained using the same set of rows for training: a logistic regression model and a random forest model with 300 trees of maximum depth 6. The two models used balanced class weights since readmissions account for only 14% of all patients. The performance of the two models on the test data was very similar: the logistic regression achieved an AUC of 0.639 and found 62% of the readmissions, while the random forest had an AUC of 0.636 and found 61% of the readmissions. The age rule managed an AUC of 0.511 and 32% of readmissions.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
