\i Step 6 - Discuss limitations in the data, features, model and evaluation (the model and the evaluation).\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The data is synthetic, so the scores say nothing about real patients, and with only ten patient measurements (age, sex, blood pressure, glucose and a few conditions) it leaves out the things that usually drive readmission, such as prior admissions, length of stay and diagnosis. The features are simple, and in testing age and glucose alone carried no signal. The model is a plain logistic regression that can only draw straight-line relationships, and the signal is weak: about four in five flagged patients are not readmitted. The evaluation uses a single 80/20 split, so the AUC of 0.639 could move a few points either way (roughly 0.60 to 0.68), and it cannot test over time or across hospitals because there is no date or site column, and fairness is checked by sex and age band only, with no ethnicity or Indigenous status data.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
