\i Step 1 - Pipeline architecture diagram: describe the reusable prediction workflow.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The prediction workflow does not train any models. It is a separate workflow from training, run by predict.py. The script loads the saved model, which has the feature step built in. New patients are treated exactly the same way the training data was. It checks that a named person has signed off on the model before it can score. After approval, the model scores a new patients CSV file containing only the feature columns and writes the results. It compares them with the excluded answers for the 484 new patients. serve.py runs the same model from a web form and API (using FastAPI). serve.py performs a sign-off check using the same signoff file. It rejects any patient whose fields do not match config.yaml.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 1 - Pipeline architecture diagram: describe monitoring considerations.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i Monitoring covers the pipeline and the model file. It doesn\u8217?t cover live performance yet. All runs of train, evaluate and compare are logged to MLflow using data and config fingerprints, and Git commit ID. A change in results is traced to what changed. The validation procedure runs 11 checks on the data before training is performed. The serve.py file has health and information endpoints. The information endpoint returns the model card. The sign-off check ensures that only approved models that have not changed since sign-off are run. monitor.py is a first drift check, run by hand on a new batch, and it reports PASS, WATCH or ALERT for input drift, data quality and the share flagged. Tracking real readmission outcomes against predictions is not built yet.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 6 - Explain what should be monitored if the system were used beyond the project.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i Monitoring would cover the inputs, checking for drift, and the share of patients flagged, which was about 39% in testing. Each month, flagged patients would be compared with those actually readmitted. Recall and precision by sex and age band, and staff response to the model's flagged patients, would also be tracked. A decline in these metrics would trigger a review and retraining.  A new sign-off is required if retraining happens.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
