\i Step 5 - Demonstrate that the prediction workflow applies the same preprocessing and feature logic used during training.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i When saving the model, a single pipeline was saved. The first step of the saved pipeline is the feature function that was used in training, dfEngineerFeatures. predict.py only has to pass the raw feature columns to the pipeline, and the calculated and encoded features are automatically created. The lists of categories for the encoded features are fixed in config.yaml. New patients will always be processed with the same columns as the training rows. Unknown categories are treated as an error and are not scored quietly. serve.py loads the same pipeline as is used for prediction.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 5 - Explain what would be needed to extend the workflow into a production deployment.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i To get the end-to-end pipeline and the FastAPI service into production, some additional controls are needed. This starts with using real clinical data and clinical validation. The service can then be containerised and protected with authentication and encryption. Model and dataset management is realised via a model registry and a DVC remote. The simple JSON sign-off is replaced by a tamper-evident record. Monitoring and retraining from Step 6 is added. Privacy and regulatory approval for scoring real patients needs to be obtained before this can happen.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\par
\i Step 6 - Identify operational risks, such as missing inputs, model or data drift, and predictions outside expected ranges.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
\par
\b \i Answer:\b0\i0\ulnone  \i The model does not know how to handle missing inputs, so it stops with an error. The service also refuses to process incomplete patients, so a data feed that started to send blanks would stop scoring. A complete but implausible record (e.g., a 400-year-old patient) still gets a score from predict and the service, but monitor.py flags values outside the validation ranges when run on a batch. Drift is only checked when monitor.py is run by hand, so a drop in recall could go unnoticed until real outcomes are compared with predictions.\b0\i0\ulnone \par
